%I A399020 #14 Aug 21 2026 00:31:39
%S A399020 3,5,13,17,23,107,139,397,953,2441,7591,39791
%N A399020 Primes p such that (p#/2)^2 - 2 is prime, where p# is the primorial of p.
%C A399020 Here p#/2 is the product of the odd primes up to p. Every prime q <= p divides p#/2, so no such q can divide (p#/2)^2 - 2, and every prime divisor of the term is congruent to +-1 (mod 8), since 2 must be a quadratic residue modulo any divisor.
%e A399020 13 is a term because 3*5*7*11*13 = 15015 and 15015^2 - 2 = 225450223 is prime.
%t A399020 primorial[n_]:=Product[Prime[i], {i, n}]; Prime[Select[Range[500], PrimeQ[(primorial[#]/2)^2-2] &]] (* _Stefano Spezia_, Aug 16 2026 *)
%o A399020 (PARI) my(M=1); forprime(p=3, 8000, M*=p; if(ispseudoprime(M^2-2), print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A399020 Cf. A002110, A070826, A005234, A014545, A057704.
%K A399020 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A399020 1,1
%A A399020 _Hossein Sadeghpour_, Aug 16 2026
